{"id":"neet-2026r-q071","pn":71,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Magnetic moment of complexes","q":"Among the species given below, the spin-only magnetic moment is highest for\n(Given: Atomic number of Ti = 22, Mn = 25, Fe = 26 and Co = 27)","o":["[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻"],"c":3,"s":"Spin-only magnetic moment is highest when the number of unpaired electrons is highest.\n[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺: Ti³⁺ = [Ar] 4s⁰ 3d¹, so unpaired electrons = 1.\n[Mn(CN)₆]³⁻: Mn³⁺ = [Ar] 4s⁰ 3d⁴, CN⁻ is a strong ligand so the 4 electrons go into t₂g (2 paired, 2 unpaired), so unpaired electrons = 2.\n[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻: Fe³⁺ = [Ar] 4s⁰ 3d⁵, low spin t₂g⁵, so unpaired electrons = 1.\nHighest is [Mn(CN)₆]³⁻ with 2 unpaired electrons.\nNote: the printed solution figure shows only four unpaired electrons for Fe³⁺ (3d⁵); a d⁵ ion has five, so the figure here shows five.\nNote: Publisher's solution does not show Co³⁺ (0 unpaired electrons, low spin d⁶); answer unaffected.","sfig":{"w":250,"h":190,"alt":"Crystal field splitting with strong-field CN⁻: singly filled d boxes (four for Mn³⁺, five for Fe³⁺) split into empty eg and lower t₂g; for Mn³⁺ the t₂g boxes hold paired, single, single electrons; for Fe³⁺ they hold paired, paired, single.","d":[["t",10,14,"Mn³⁺ (3d⁴)","s"],["r",10,36,14,18,""],["p","M17 51L17 39L14 43",""],["r",27,36,14,18,""],["p","M34 51L34 39L31 43",""],["r",44,36,14,18,""],["p","M51 51L51 39L48 43",""],["r",61,36,14,18,""],["p","M68 51L68 39L65 43",""],["l",75,45,85,45,""],["l",85,45,105,18,""],["l",105,18,190,18,""],["t",194,16,"eg","s"],["l",85,45,109,74,""],["l",109,74,170,74,""],["r",170,65,14,18,""],["p","M175 80L175 68L172 72",""],["p","M179 68L179 80L182 76",""],["r",187,65,14,18,""],["p","M194 80L194 68L191 72",""],["r",204,65,14,18,""],["p","M211 80L211 68L208 72",""],["t",224,82,"t₂g","s"],["t",10,109,"Fe³⁺ (3d⁵)","s"],["r",10,131,14,18,""],["p","M17 146L17 134L14 138",""],["r",27,131,14,18,""],["p","M34 146L34 134L31 138",""],["r",44,131,14,18,""],["p","M51 146L51 134L48 138",""],["r",61,131,14,18,""],["p","M68 146L68 134L65 138",""],["r",78,131,14,18,""],["p","M85 146L85 134L82 138",""],["l",92,140,102,140,""],["l",102,140,122,113,""],["l",122,113,190,113,""],["t",194,111,"eg","s"],["l",102,140,126,169,""],["l",126,169,170,169,""],["r",170,160,14,18,""],["p","M175 175L175 163L172 167",""],["p","M179 163L179 175L182 171",""],["r",187,160,14,18,""],["p","M192 175L192 163L189 167",""],["p","M196 163L196 175L199 171",""],["r",204,160,14,18,""],["p","M211 175L211 163L208 167",""],["t",224,177,"t₂g","s"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q072","pn":72,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Isomerism in coordination compounds","q":"The complex which has facial and meridional isomers is\n(Given : py = pyridine and en = H₂N – CH₂ – CH₂ – NH₂)","o":["[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]³⁺","[Ni(en)₂(H₂O)₂]²⁺","[Cr(py)₃(Cl)₃]"],"c":3,"s":"Complexes of the type [Ma₃b₃] show facial and meridional isomers.\nSo [Cr(py)₃(Cl)₃] shows fac and mer isomers."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q073","pn":73,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Ionisation of coordination compounds","q":"A 1 : 3 electrolyte in an aqueous solution is","o":["[CoCl(NH₃)₅]Cl₂","[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃","[Co(NH₃)₃(NO₂)₃]","[CoCl₂(NH₃)₄]Cl"],"c":1,"s":"[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ in water dissociates into [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and 3Cl⁻ ions.\nSo it is a 1 : 3 electrolyte."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q078","pn":78,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Nomenclature of coordination compounds","q":"The formula of tetraammineaquachloridocobalt(III) chloride is","o":["[Co(NH₃)₄]Cl₃ × H₂O","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Cl]Cl","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Cl]Cl₂","[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂] × H₂O"],"c":2,"s":"Ligands: 4 ammine (NH₃), 1 aqua (H₂O), 1 chlorido (Cl⁻); central metal Co³⁺.\nCharge on the complex ion = +3 − 1 = +2, so two Cl⁻ outside the bracket.\nFormula: [Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Cl]Cl₂."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q082","pn":82,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Hybridisation and shape of complexes","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. [Pt(Cl₂)(NH₃)₂] — I. Octahedral\nB. [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ — II. Trigonal bipyramidal\nC. [NiCl₄]²⁻ — III. Square planar\nD. [Fe(CO)₅] — IV. Tetrahedral\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"c":3,"s":"[Pt(Cl₂)(NH₃)₂]: Pt has dsp² hybridisation, so the shape is square planar (A-III).\n[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃: Co has d²sp³ hybridisation, so the shape is octahedral (B-I).\n[NiCl₄]²⁻: Ni has sp³ hybridisation, so the shape is tetrahedral (C-IV).\n[Fe(CO)₅]: the shape is trigonal bipyramidal (D-II)."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q084","pn":84,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Ligands and denticity","q":"Which one of the following is an ambidentate ligand?","o":["Ethylenediaminetetraacetate ion","Oxalate","Ethane-1,2-diamine","Thiocyanate"],"c":3,"s":"An ambidentate ligand has two different donor atoms, and either one can bond to the metal in a complex.\n(1) Ethylenediaminetetraacetate ion is hexadentate.\n(2) Oxalate is didentate.\n(3) Ethane-1,2-diamine is didentate.\n(4) Thiocyanate (SCN⁻) is an ambidentate ligand."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q090","pn":90,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Isomerism in complexes","q":"Match List I with List II :\nA. [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] — I. Optical\nB. [Co(en)₃]³⁺ — II. Solvate\nC. [Co(NH₃)₅NO₂]Cl₂ — III. Geometrical\nD. [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ — IV. Linkage\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"c":2,"s":"A. [Pt(NH₃)₂Cl₂] has cis and trans forms, so the isomerism is geometrical.\nB. [Co(en)₃]³⁺ has non-superimposable mirror images, so it shows optical isomerism.\nC. [Co(NH₃)₅NO₂]Cl₂ and [Co(NH₃)₅(ONO)]Cl₂ are linkage isomers.\nD. [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ and [Cr(H₂O)₅Cl]Cl₂·H₂O are solvate isomers.\nSo A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.","sfig":{"w":345,"h":210,"alt":"A: cis and trans square planar [Pt(NH3)2Cl2], geometrical isomers. B: two octahedral [Co(en)3]3+ structures with N–N chelate arcs drawn as mirror images, optical isomers; en shown as N–N.","d":[["t",2,34,"A.",""],["t",60,34,"Pt","m"],["l",52,25,38,15,""],["l",52,35,38,45,""],["l",69,25,83,15,""],["l",69,35,83,45,""],["t",30,16,"Cl","m"],["t",30,54,"Cl","m"],["t",84,15,"NH₃",""],["t",84,52,"NH₃",""],["t",130,34,"and",""],["t",220,34,"Pt","m"],["l",212,25,198,15,""],["l",212,35,198,45,""],["l",229,25,243,15,""],["l",229,35,243,45,""],["t",190,16,"Cl","m"],["t",190,54,"NH₃","m"],["t",244,15,"NH₃",""],["t",244,52,"Cl",""],["t",26,72,"⇒ Geometrical isomers",""],["t",2,152,"B.",""],["t",72,156,"Co","m"],["t",72,112,"N","ms"],["l",72,140.1,72,120.3,""],["t",72,200,"N","ms"],["l",72,163.9,72,183.7,""],["t",36,132,"N","ms"],["l",62.3,145.5,46.1,134.7,""],["t",108,132,"N","ms"],["l",81.7,145.5,97.9,134.7,""],["t",36,180,"N","ms"],["l",62.3,158.5,46.1,169.3,""],["t",108,180,"N","ms"],["l",81.7,158.5,97.9,169.3,""],["p","M76 102Q112 96 112 122",""],["p","M32 128Q14 152 32 176",""],["p","M76 202Q112 208 112 182",""],["t",58,92,"trans","s"],["l",102,96,112,96,""],["l",112,96,112,116,""],["t",114,100,"3+","s"],["l",134,100,134,200,"d"],["t",196,156,"Co","m"],["t",196,112,"N","ms"],["l",196,140.1,196,120.3,""],["t",196,200,"N","ms"],["l",196,163.9,196,183.7,""],["t",160,132,"N","ms"],["l",186.3,145.5,170.1,134.7,""],["t",232,132,"N","ms"],["l",205.7,145.5,221.9,134.7,""],["t",160,180,"N","ms"],["l",186.3,158.5,170.1,169.3,""],["t",232,180,"N","ms"],["l",205.7,158.5,221.9,169.3,""],["p","M156 122Q156 96 192 102",""],["p","M236 128Q254 152 236 176",""],["p","M156 182Q156 208 192 202",""],["t",182,92,"cis","s"],["l",226,96,236,96,""],["l",236,96,236,116,""],["t",238,100,"3+","s"],["t",246,156,"⇒ Optical isomers","s"],["l",262,96,258,96,""],["l",258,96,258,118,""],["l",258,118,262,118,""],["t",272,115,"N","ms"],["p","M274 100Q284 90 294 100",""],["t",296,115,"N","ms"],["t",306,111,"= en","s"],["l",334,96,338,96,""],["l",338,96,338,118,""],["l",338,118,334,118,""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2025-q051","pn":51,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Magnetic behaviour of complexes","q":"Which of the following are paramagnetic?\nA. [NiCl₄]²⁻\nB. Ni(CO)₄\nC. [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻\nD. [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺\nE. Ni(PPh₃)₄\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A and C only","B and E only","A and D only","A, D and E only"],"c":2,"s":"A. [NiCl₄]²⁻: Ni²⁺, 3d⁸, sp³ hybridisation, 2 unpaired electrons, paramagnetic.\nB. Ni(CO)₄: Ni, 3d⁸4s², sp³, zero unpaired electrons, diamagnetic.\nC. [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻: Ni²⁺, 3d⁸, dsp², zero unpaired electrons, diamagnetic.\nD. [Ni(H₂O)₆]²⁺: Ni²⁺, 3d⁸, sp³d², two unpaired electrons, paramagnetic.\nE. Ni(PPh₃)₄: Ni, 3d⁸4s², sp³, zero unpaired electrons, diamagnetic.\nSo A and D are paramagnetic."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q057","pn":57,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Crystal field splitting and colour","q":"The correct order of the wavelength of light absorbed by the following complexes is,\nA. [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺\nB. [Co(CN)₆]³⁻\nC. [Cu(H₂O)₄]²⁺\nD. [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["B < D < A < C","B < A < D < C","C < D < A < B","C < A < D < B"],"c":1,"s":"λ ∝ 1/(strength of ligand), so λ ∝ 1/splitting.\nA. [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺: 475 nm.\nB. [Co(CN)₆]³⁻: 310 nm.\nC. [Cu(H₂O)₄]²⁺: 600 nm.\nD. [Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺: 498 nm.\nOrder of λ = C > D > A > B."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q063","pn":63,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Conductance of complexes","q":"Out of the following complex compounds, which of the compound will be having the minimum conductance in solution?","o":["[Co(NH₃)₃Cl₃]","[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]","[Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃","[Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl"],"a":"Answer (1, 2)","s":"Conductance depends on the number of ions produced by the complex.\nIf the number of ions is the same, check the charge on the complex unit.\n(1) [Co⁺³(NH₃)₃Cl₃] and (2) [Co⁺²(NH₃)₄Cl₂]: both complex units have no charge, so both have the same (minimum) conductance.\n(3) [Co(NH₃)₆]Cl₃ → [Co(NH₃)₆]⁺³ + 3Cl⁻.\n(4) [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]Cl → [Co(NH₃)₅Cl]⁺ + Cl⁻.\nNote: Printed answer lists two options, 1 and 2 (both non-ionic); no single index"},
{"id":"neet-2024-q064","pn":64,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Magnetic behaviour and hybridisation of complexes","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement-I : Both [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ and [CoF₆]³⁻ complexes are octahedral but differ in their magnetic behaviour.\nStatement-II : [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is diamagnetic whereas [CoF₆]³⁻ is paramagnetic.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["Statement I true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"c":2,"s":"In [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺, Co³⁺ is 3d⁶.\nNH₃ is a strong field ligand, so the electrons pair up in 3d.\nTwo 3d orbitals, the 4s and three 4p orbitals form d²sp³ hybrids, so the complex is octahedral and diamagnetic.\nIn [CoF₆]³⁻, Co³⁺ is also 3d⁶.\nF⁻ is a weak field ligand, so no pairing occurs.\nThe complex is sp³d² hybridised (using 4s, 4p, 4d), octahedral, with four unpaired electrons, so it is paramagnetic.\nBoth statements are true.","sfig":{"w":380,"h":216,"alt":"Orbital box diagrams of Co3+ with NH3 (d2sp3, all paired) and with F- (sp3d2, four unpaired electrons)","d":[["t",6,14,"Co³⁺ (free ion)","s"],["t",130,14,"3d⁶","s"],["t",62.5,55,"3d","ms"],["r",10,22,21,21,""],["t",20.5,35.5,"↑↓","ms"],["r",31,22,21,21,""],["t",41.5,35.5,"↑","ms"],["r",52,22,21,21,""],["t",62.5,35.5,"↑","ms"],["r",73,22,21,21,""],["t",83.5,35.5,"↑","ms"],["r",94,22,21,21,""],["t",104.5,35.5,"↑","ms"],["t",137.5,55,"4s","ms"],["r",127,22,21,21,""],["t",191.5,55,"4p","ms"],["r",160,22,21,21,""],["r",181,22,21,21,""],["r",202,22,21,21,""],["t",6,80,"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","s"],["t",130,80,"d²sp³: diamagnetic","s"],["t",62.5,121,"3d","ms"],["r",10,88,21,21,""],["t",20.5,101.5,"↑↓","ms"],["r",31,88,21,21,""],["t",41.5,101.5,"↑↓","ms"],["r",52,88,21,21,""],["t",62.5,101.5,"↑↓","ms"],["r",73,88,21,21,""],["t",83.5,101.5,"××","ms"],["r",94,88,21,21,""],["t",104.5,101.5,"××","ms"],["t",137.5,121,"4s","ms"],["r",127,88,21,21,""],["t",137.5,101.5,"××","ms"],["t",191.5,121,"4p","ms"],["r",160,88,21,21,""],["t",170.5,101.5,"××","ms"],["r",181,88,21,21,""],["t",191.5,101.5,"××","ms"],["r",202,88,21,21,""],["t",212.5,101.5,"××","ms"],["t",6,146,"[CoF₆]³⁻","s"],["t",130,146,"sp³d²: 4 unpaired e⁻","s"],["t",62.5,187,"3d","ms"],["r",10,154,21,21,""],["t",20.5,167.5,"↑↓","ms"],["r",31,154,21,21,""],["t",41.5,167.5,"↑","ms"],["r",52,154,21,21,""],["t",62.5,167.5,"↑","ms"],["r",73,154,21,21,""],["t",83.5,167.5,"↑","ms"],["r",94,154,21,21,""],["t",104.5,167.5,"↑","ms"],["t",137.5,187,"4s","ms"],["r",127,154,21,21,""],["t",137.5,167.5,"××","ms"],["t",191.5,187,"4p","ms"],["r",160,154,21,21,""],["t",170.5,167.5,"××","ms"],["r",181,154,21,21,""],["t",191.5,167.5,"××","ms"],["r",202,154,21,21,""],["t",212.5,167.5,"××","ms"],["t",287.5,187,"4d","ms"],["r",235,154,21,21,""],["t",245.5,167.5,"××","ms"],["r",256,154,21,21,""],["t",266.5,167.5,"××","ms"],["t",6,206,"×× = electron pair donated by a ligand","s"],["r",277,154,21,21,""],["r",298,154,21,21,""],["r",319,154,21,21,""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2024-q080","pn":80,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Isomerism in coordination compounds","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Complex) — List-II (Type of isomerism)\nA. [Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ — I. Solvate isomerism\nB. [Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br — II. Linkage isomerism\nC. [Co(NH₃)₆][Cr(CN)₆] — III. Ionization isomerism\nD. [Co(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ — IV. Coordination isomerism\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below :","o":["A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"c":2,"s":"A: [Co(NH₃)₅(NO₂)]Cl₂ shows linkage isomerism, since NO₂ can bind through N or through O. So A-II.\nB: [Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br shows ionization isomerism. So B-III.\nC: [Co(NH₃)₆][Cr(CN)₆] shows coordination isomerism. So C-IV.\nD: [Co(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ shows solvate (hydrate) isomerism. So D-I."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q090","pn":90,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Homoleptic and heteroleptic complexes","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement-I : [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ is a homoleptic complex whereas [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ is a heteroleptic complex.\nStatement-II : Complex [Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ has only one kind of ligands but [Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ has more than one kind of ligands.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["Statement I true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"c":2,"s":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺ has only one type of ligand (NH₃) on Co³⁺, so it is homoleptic.\n[Co(NH₃)₄Cl₂]⁺ has more than one type of ligand on Co³⁺, namely NH₃ and Cl⁻, so it is heteroleptic.\nBoth statements are true."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q085","pn":85,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Homoleptic and heteroleptic complexes","q":"Homoleptic complex from the following complexes is :","o":["Triamminetriaquachromium (III) chloride","Potassium trioxalatoaluminate (III)","Diamminechloridonitrito-N-platinum (II)","Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt (III) chloride"],"c":1,"s":"A homoleptic complex has the metal bound to only one kind of donor group.\nPotassium trioxalatoaluminate (III), K₃[Al(ox)₃], has only oxalate ligands.\nSo it is homoleptic."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q090","pn":90,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Chelate effect","q":"Which complex compound is most stable?","o":["[Co(NH₃)₆]₂(SO₄)₃","[Co(NH₃)₄(H₂O)Br](NO₃)₂","[Co(NH₃)₃(NO₃)₃]","[CoCl₂(en)₂]NO₃"],"c":3,"s":"Chelating ligands usually form more stable complexes than similar monodentate ligands.\nOnly [CoCl₂(en)₂]NO₃ contains a chelating ligand (en).\nSo it is the most stable."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q065","pn":65,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Isomerism in complexes","q":"Match List - I with List - II :\nList-I (Complexes): (a) [Co(NH₃)₅NO₂]Cl₂ and [Co(NH₃)₅ONO]Cl₂ (b) [Cr(NH₃)₆][Co(CN)₆] and [Cr(CN)₆][Co(NH₃)₆] (c) [Co(NH₃)₅(SO₄)]Br and [Co(NH₃)₅Br]SO₄ (d) [Cr(H₂O)₆]Cl₃ and [Cr(H₂O)₅Cl]Cl₂·H₂O\nList-II (Types): (i) ionisation isomerism (ii) coordination isomerism (iii) linkage isomerism (iv) solvate isomerism\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (i), (c) - (ii), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)"],"c":3,"s":"(a) NO₂ and ONO are two ways an ambidentate ligand can bind, so it is linkage isomerism.\n(b) Ligands are exchanged between the cationic and anionic complex, so it is coordination isomerism.\n(c) SO₄²⁻ and Br⁻ swap between inside and outside the sphere, giving different ions in solution, so it is ionisation isomerism.\n(d) The number of water molecules as ligand and as water of crystallisation differs, so it is solvate isomerism."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q066","pn":66,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"IUPAC naming of complexes","q":"The IUPAC name of the complex- [Ag(H₂O)₂][Ag(CN)₂] is:","o":["diaquasilver(II) dicyanidoargentate(II)","dicyanidosilver(I) diaquaargentate(I)","diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate(I)","dicyanidosilver(II) diaquaargentate(II)"],"c":2,"s":"The cation is [Ag(H₂O)₂]⁺: two water ligands, silver in +1 state, named diaquasilver(I).\nThe anion is [Ag(CN)₂]⁻: two cyanide ligands, named dicyanidoargentate(I).\nThe name is diaquasilver(I) dicyanidoargentate(I)."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q086","pn":86,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Crystal field theory and colour","q":"The order of energy absorbed which is responsible for the color of complexes\n(A) [Ni(H₂O)₂(en)₂]²⁺\n(B) [Ni(H₂O)₄(en)]²⁺ and\n(C) [Ni(en)₃]²⁺\nis","o":["(C) > (B) > (A)","(C) > (A) > (B)","(B) > (A) > (C)","(A) > (B) > (C)"],"c":1,"s":"A stronger ligand field gives a larger splitting, so more energy is absorbed.\nIn the spectrochemical series 'en' is stronger than H₂O.\nMore 'en' ligands means higher energy absorbed.\nOrder: [Ni(en)₃]²⁺ > [Ni(H₂O)₂(en)₂]²⁺ > [Ni(H₂O)₄(en)]²⁺, i.e. (C) > (A) > (B)."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q086","pn":86,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Metal carbonyl bonding","q":"Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).\nAssertion (A) : The metal carbon bond in metal carbonyls possesses both σ and π character.\nReason (R) : The ligand to metal bond is a π bond and metal to ligand bond is a σ bond.\nIn the Light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct"],"c":3,"s":"In metal carbonyls the M-C bond has both σ and π character, so (A) is correct.\nThe σ bond forms when the ligand donates a lone pair to the metal.\nThe π bond forms when the metal donates d electrons back to the ligand's empty π* orbital (back bonding).\nThe reason has these two reversed, so (R) is not correct.\nNote: The printed solution calls (R) correct but the printed key is 4 (R not correct); the key is kept and the solution here follows the key, since ligand to metal is the σ bond."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q077","pn":77,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"EDTA ligand","q":"Ethylene diaminetetraacetate (EDTA) ion is :","o":["Tridentate ligand with three \"N\" donor atoms","Hexadentate ligand with four \"O\" and two \"N\" donor atoms","Unidentate ligand","Bidentate ligand with two \"N\" donor atoms"],"c":1,"s":"EDTA⁴⁻ has two N atoms (amine) and four carboxylate O⁻ atoms.\nAll six can donate a lone pair to the metal, so it is hexadentate with 4 O and 2 N donors.","sfig":{"w":300,"h":235,"alt":"EDTA ion bound to a metal M: two nitrogen atoms joined by a CH2-CH2 bridge, each carrying two CH2COO- arms, with arrows from the four O- atoms and the two N atoms pointing to M","d":[["t",95,64,"O⁻","m"],["l",95,69,95,91],["t",95,104,"C","m"],["l",86,98.5,73,98.5],["l",86,101.5,73,101.5],["t",64,104,"O","m"],["t",48,92,"O⁻","m"],["l",48,97,48,109],["t",48,122,"C","m"],["l",39,116.5,26,116.5],["l",39,119.5,26,119.5],["t",17,122,"O","m"],["l",95,109,95,130],["t",95,146,"CH₂","m"],["l",95,152,95,166],["t",95,178,"N","m"],["l",48,127,48,160],["t",48,174,"CH₂","m"],["l",63,174,86,178],["l",92,186,109,203],["l",99,168,139,84,"a"],["p","M103 61 Q125 44 138 66","a"],["p","M56 86 C78 20 140 20 138 63","a"],["t",205,64,"O⁻","m"],["l",205,69,205,91],["t",205,104,"C","m"],["l",214,98.5,227,98.5],["l",214,101.5,227,101.5],["t",236,104,"O","m"],["t",252,92,"O⁻","m"],["l",252,97,252,109],["t",252,122,"C","m"],["l",261,116.5,274,116.5],["l",261,119.5,274,119.5],["t",283,122,"O","m"],["l",205,109,205,130],["t",205,146,"CH₂","m"],["l",205,152,205,166],["t",205,178,"N","m"],["l",252,127,252,160],["t",252,174,"CH₂","m"],["l",237,174,214,178],["l",208,186,191,203],["l",201,168,161,84,"a"],["p","M197 61 Q175 44 162 66","a"],["p","M244 86 C222 20 160 20 162 63","a"],["t",150,72,"M","m"],["t",150,216,"CH₂–CH₂","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2021-q087","pn":87,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Magnetic moment of complexes","q":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I: (a) [Fe(CN)₆]³⁻ (b) [Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ (c) [Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻ (d) [Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺\nList-II: (i) 5.92 BM (ii) 0 BM (iii) 4.90 BM (iv) 1.73 BM","o":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"c":0,"s":"μ = √(n(n + 2)) BM, n = unpaired electrons. CN⁻ is a strong field ligand (pairs electrons); H₂O is weak (does not pair).\n(a) Fe³⁺ is d⁵, low spin: n = 1, μ = 1.73 BM.\n(b) Fe³⁺ d⁵, high spin: n = 5, μ = 5.92 BM.\n(c) Fe²⁺ d⁶, low spin: n = 0, μ = 0.\n(d) Fe²⁺ d⁶, high spin: n = 4, μ = 4.90 BM.\nMatch: a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q146","pn":146,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Complex formation","q":"Urea reacts with water to form A which will decompose to form B. B when passed through Cu²⁺(aq), deep blue colour solution C is formed. What is the formula of C from the following ?","o":["[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺","Cu(OH)₂","CuCO₃.Cu(OH)₂","CuSO₄"],"c":0,"s":"Urea + water gives ammonium carbonate-type product A (NH₄OH with CO₂).\nA decomposes on heating to give ammonia, B = NH₃.\nCu²⁺ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺, which is deep blue."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q174","pn":174,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Spectrochemical series","q":"Which of the following is the correct order of increasing field strength of ligands to form coordination compounds ?","o":["SCN⁻ < F⁻ < CN⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻","F⁻ < SCN⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < CN⁻","CN⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < SCN⁻ < F⁻","SCN⁻ < F⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < CN⁻"],"c":3,"s":"The spectrochemical series in increasing field strength is SCN⁻ < F⁻ < C₂O₄²⁻ < CN⁻ (weak to strong)."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q173","pn":173,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Crystal field theory","q":"What is the correct electronic configuration of the central atom in K₄[Fe(CN)₆] based on crystal field theory?","o":["e³t₂³","e⁴t₂²","t₂g⁴ e_g²","t₂g⁶ e_g⁰"],"c":3,"s":"The complex K₄[Fe(CN)₆] has Fe²⁺ with 3d⁶.\nCN⁻ is a strong field ligand, so pairing happens in the octahedral field.\nAll six d electrons go in the lower t₂g set: t₂g⁶ e_g⁰."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q083","pn":83,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Isomerism in coordination compounds","q":"The type of isomerism shown by the complex [CoCl₂(en)₂] is -","o":["Geometrical isomerism","Coordination isomerism","Ionization isomerism","Linkage isomerism"],"c":0,"s":"The complex is octahedral of type [M(AA)₂X₂] with en as a bidentate ligand.\nThe two Cl⁻ can be next to each other (cis) or opposite (trans).\nSo it shows geometrical isomerism (cis and trans forms).","sfig":{"w":330,"h":165,"alt":"cis and trans forms of Co with two en ligands and two Cl; in cis the Cl are adjacent, in trans they are opposite.","d":[["r",32,49,78,48,""],["l",32,49,57,64,""],["l",32,97,57,82,""],["l",110,49,85,64,""],["l",110,97,85,82,""],["t",71,78,"Co","ms"],["l",71,49,71,17,""],["t",71,12,"Cl","m"],["l",110,49,119,40,""],["t",121,42,"Cl",""],["t",3,78,"en",""],["p","M32 49C19 55 19 91 32 97",""],["p","M108 97V108Q108 113 100 113",""],["t",97,126,"en",""],["t",71,152,"cis","m"],["r",203,49,80,48,""],["l",203,49,228,64,""],["l",203,97,228,82,""],["l",283,49,258,64,""],["l",283,97,258,82,""],["t",243,78,"Co","ms"],["l",243,49,243,17,""],["t",243,12,"Cl","m"],["l",243,97,243,121,""],["t",243,134,"Cl","m"],["p","M203 49C190 55 190 91 203 97",""],["p","M283 49C296 55 296 91 283 97",""],["t",187,78,"en","e"],["t",301,78,"en",""],["t",243,152,"trans","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2018-q085","pn":85,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Metal carbonyls","q":"The geometry and magnetic behavior of the complex [Ni(CO)₄] are","o":["square planar geometry and diamagnetic","tetrahedral geometry and diamagnetic","square planar geometry and paramagnetic","tetrahedral geometry and paramagnetic"],"c":1,"s":"Ni is 3d⁸4s² and CO is a strong-field ligand, so the 4s electrons move into 3d, giving 3d¹⁰.\nAll electrons are paired, so it is diamagnetic.\nNi uses one 4s and three 4p orbitals (sp³), so the shape is tetrahedral."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q086","pn":86,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Metal carbonyls","q":"Iron carbonyl, Fe(CO)₅ is","o":["tetranuclear","mononuclear","trinuclear","dinuclear"],"c":1,"s":"Fe(CO)₅ has only one metal atom (Fe), so it is mononuclear.\nIts shape is trigonal bipyramidal."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q087","pn":87,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Spin-only magnetic moment","q":"Match the metal ions given in column I with the spin magnetic moments of the ions given in Column II and assign the correct code - Column I: (a) Co³⁺ (b) Cr³⁺ (c) Fe³⁺ (d) Ni²⁺. Column II: (i) √8 B.M. (ii) √35 B.M. (iii) √3 B.M. (iv) √24 B.M. (v) √15 B.M.","o":["a-iv, b-v, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii","a-iii, b-v, c-i, d-ii"],"c":0,"s":"Spin-only moment μ = √(n(n+2)) B.M., n = number of unpaired electrons.\nCo³⁺ is 3d⁶ (high spin), n = 4, μ = √24.\nCr³⁺ is 3d³, n = 3, μ = √15.\nFe³⁺ is 3d⁵, n = 5, μ = √35.\nNi²⁺ is 3d⁸, n = 2, μ = √8.\nMatch: a–iv, b–v, c–ii, d–i."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q014","pn":14,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Hybridisation in complexes","q":"Pick out the correct statement with respect to [Mn(CN)6]³⁻ :","o":["It is dsp² hybridised and square planar","It is sp³d² hybridised and octahedral","It is sp³d² hybridised and tetrahedral","It is d²sp³ hybridised and octahedral"],"c":3,"s":"Mn is 3d⁵4s², so Mn³⁺ is 3d⁴.\nCN⁻ is a strong field ligand, so the four d electrons pair up in the t2g orbitals, leaving two 3d orbitals empty.\nTwo 3d, one 4s and three 4p orbitals mix to give d²sp³ hybrids.\nSix CN⁻ bond to it, so the shape is octahedral (inner orbital complex).","sfig":{"w":340,"h":130,"alt":"Orbital boxes of Mn³⁺ 3d⁴ with four unpaired electrons splitting into two empty-pair pattern for d²sp³ inner octahedral hybridisation, n = 2","d":[["r",10,50,20,20,""],["t",20,64,"↑","m"],["r",30,50,20,20,""],["t",40,64,"↑","m"],["r",50,50,20,20,""],["t",60,64,"↑","m"],["r",70,50,20,20,""],["t",80,64,"↑","m"],["r",90,50,20,20,""],["t",100,64,"","m"],["l",112,60,150,60,""],["l",150,60,175,28,""],["l",150,60,175,92,""],["r",180,8,20,20,""],["t",190,22,"↑","m"],["r",180,82,20,20,""],["t",190,96,"⇅","m"],["r",200,82,20,20,""],["t",210,96,"↑","m"],["r",220,82,20,20,""],["t",230,96,"↑","m"],["t",215,16,"d² sp³",""],["t",215,32,"inner octahedral",""],["t",215,48,"n = 2",""]]}},
{"id":"neet-2017-q023","pn":23,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Ionisation isomerism and AgCl","q":"The correct order of the stoichiometries of AgCl formed when AgNO3 in excess is treated with the complexes : CoCl3.6NH3, CoCl3.5NH3, CoCl3.4NH3 respectively is -","o":["2AgCl, 3AgCl, 1AgCl","1AgCl, 3AgCl, 2AgCl","3AgCl, 1AgCl, 2AgCl","3AgCl, 2AgCl, 1AgCl"],"c":3,"s":"Only free Cl⁻ outside the coordination sphere precipitates with AgNO3.\nCoCl3.6NH3 = [Co(NH3)6]Cl3 has 3 free Cl⁻, so 3AgCl.\nCoCl3.5NH3 = [Co(NH3)5Cl]Cl2 has 2 free Cl⁻, so 2AgCl.\nCoCl3.4NH3 = [Co(NH3)4Cl2]Cl has 1 free Cl⁻, so 1AgCl."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q024","pn":24,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Organometallic compounds","q":"An example of a sigma bonded organometallic compound is -","o":["Cobaltocene","Ruthenocene","Grignard's reagent","Ferrocene"],"c":2,"s":"Grignard's reagent R-Mg-X has a direct metal-carbon sigma bond (for example CH3-Mg-I).\nFerrocene, cobaltocene and ruthenocene hold cyclopentadienyl rings by pi bonds, so they are pi-bonded."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q034","pn":34,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Crystal field splitting and colour","q":"Correct increasing order for the wavelengths of absorption in the visible region for the complexes of Co³⁺ is -","o":["[Co(NH3)6]³⁺, [Co(en)3]³⁺, [Co(H2O)6]³⁺","[Co(en)3]³⁺, [Co(NH3)6]³⁺, [Co(H2O)6]³⁺","[Co(H2O)6]³⁺, [Co(en)3]³⁺, [Co(NH3)6]³⁺","[Co(H2O)6]³⁺, [Co(NH3)6]³⁺, [Co(en)3]³⁺"],"c":1,"s":"Ligand field strength order: en > NH3 > H2O.\nSo splitting Δ0 is largest for en and smallest for H2O.\nΔ0 = hc/λ, so a larger Δ0 means a shorter wavelength absorbed.\nWavelength order: [Co(en)3]³⁺ < [Co(NH3)6]³⁺ < [Co(H2O)6]³⁺."},
{"id":"neet-2016-q031","pn":31,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 1 May","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Metal carbonyl bonding","q":"Which of the following has longest C – O bond length ? (Free C – O bond length in CO is 1.128 Å)","o":["[Co(CO)₄]⁻","[Fe(CO)₄]²⁻","[Mn(CO)₆]⁺","Ni(CO)₄"],"c":1,"s":"In metal carbonyls the metal donates electrons back into the π* orbital of CO (back bonding).\nMore back bonding means a lower C–O bond order and a longer C–O bond.\nOxidation states: Ni(CO)₄ Ni⁰, [Mn(CO)₆]⁺ Mn⁺, [Co(CO)₄]⁻ Co⁻¹, [Fe(CO)₄]²⁻ Fe⁻².\nFe⁻² has the most negative charge, so the most electron density to give back to CO.\nSo [Fe(CO)₄]²⁻ has the longest C–O bond.\nNote: Options (1) and (2) are printed without their negative charges; the charges are written as in the solution, for example [Co(CO)₄]⁻."},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q062","pn":62,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Complex formation","q":"AlF₃ is soluble in HF only in presence of KF. It is due to the formation of","o":["K₃[AlF₃H₃]","K₃[AlF₆]","AlH₃","K[AlF₃H]"],"c":1,"s":"KF supplies F⁻ ions.\nAlF₃ is insoluble, but it adds three more F⁻ to form the soluble complex ion [AlF₆]³⁻.\nAlF₃ + 3KF → K₃[AlF₆]."},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q073","pn":73,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Trans effect","q":"The correct increasing order of trans-effect of the following species is","o":["NH₃ > CN⁻ > Br⁻ > C₆H₅⁻","CN⁻ > C₆H₅⁻ > Br⁻ > NH₃","Br⁻ > CN⁻ > NH₃ > C₆H₅⁻","CN⁻ > Br⁻ > C₆H₅⁻ > NH₃"],"c":1,"s":"The trans effect is how strongly a ligand speeds up substitution of the group opposite to it in a square planar complex.\nπ-acceptor ligands such as CN⁻ are strongest.\nThe standard series is CN⁻ > C₆H₅⁻ > Br⁻ > NH₃.\nThis matches option (2), which lists them in decreasing trans effect.\nNote: The question says 'increasing' but the key (2) lists decreasing order of trans effect; printed key kept.","sfig":{"w":320,"h":100,"alt":"Square planar complex ML2TX reacts with Nu, replacing X (trans to T) and giving ML2TNu.","d":[["t",22,22,"L","ms"],["l",28,28,44,40],["t",50,44,"M","ms"],["l",56,40,72,28],["t",78,22,"X","ms"],["l",56,50,72,62],["t",78,68,"L","ms"],["l",28,62,44,50],["t",22,68,"T","ms"],["l",110,45,170,45,"a"],["t",140,32,"Nu","ms"],["t",140,66,"–X","ms"],["t",202,22,"L","ms"],["l",208,28,224,40],["t",230,44,"M","ms"],["l",236,40,252,28],["t",262,22,"Nu","ms"],["l",236,50,252,62],["t",258,68,"L","ms"],["l",208,62,224,50],["t",202,68,"T","ms"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2016b-q075","pn":75,"y":"AIPMT 2016 · 24 Jul","tp":"Coordination Compounds","st":"Jahn-Teller effect","q":"Jahn-Teller effect is not observed in high spin complexes of","o":["d⁷","d⁸","d⁴","d⁹"],"c":1,"s":"The Jahn-Teller distortion appears when the d electrons fill the t₂g or e_g orbitals unevenly (degenerate ground state).\nd⁴ (t₂g³e_g¹) and d⁹ (t₂g⁶e_g³) have uneven e_g filling, so they distort strongly.\nd⁷ also has an uneven filling in the figure shown in the key.\nd⁸ (t₂g⁶e_g²) fills both e_g orbitals evenly, so no distortion is expected.\nNote: printed answer 2; the key's figure draws d⁷ as low spin; for high spin d⁷ (t₂g⁵e_g²) only a weak distortion arises, so d⁸ is still the best choice.","sfig":{"w":400,"h":175,"alt":"Octahedral d-electron filling: t2g and eg boxes for d4, d7, d9 (asymmetric eg filling) and d8 (symmetric filling, both eg orbitals singly occupied).","d":[["t",77,16,"t₂g","ms"],["t",194.9,16,"e",""],["t",200.9,19,"g","s"],["t",2,45,"d⁴","s"],["r",45,28,27,25],["t",58.5,45,"↑","ms"],["r",72,28,27,25],["t",85.5,45,"↑","ms"],["r",99,28,27,25],["t",112.5,45,"↑","ms"],["r",181,28,28,25],["t",195,45,"↑","ms"],["r",209,28,28,25],["t",2,82,"d⁷","s"],["r",45,65,27,25],["t",58.5,82,"↑↓","ms"],["r",72,65,27,25],["t",85.5,82,"↑↓","ms"],["r",99,65,27,25],["t",112.5,82,"↑↓","ms"],["r",181,65,28,25],["t",195,82,"↑","ms"],["r",209,65,28,25],["t",2,118,"d⁹","s"],["r",45,101,27,25],["t",58.5,118,"↑↓","ms"],["r",72,101,27,25],["t",85.5,118,"↑↓","ms"],["r",99,101,27,25],["t",112.5,118,"↑↓","ms"],["r",181,101,28,25],["t",195,118,"↑↓","ms"],["r",209,101,28,25],["t",223,118,"↑","ms"],["t",2,154,"d⁸","s"],["r",45,137,27,25],["t",58.5,154,"↑↓","ms"],["r",72,137,27,25],["t",85.5,154,"↑↓","ms"],["r",99,137,27,25],["t",112.5,154,"↑↓","ms"],["r",181,137,28,25],["t",195,154,"↑","ms"],["r",209,137,28,25],["t",223,154,"↑","ms"],["p","M243 28 Q249 28 249 40 V95 Q249 128 243 128"],["p","M249 77 H254"],["t",263,83,"(asymmetric filling)","s"],["t",250,152,"Symmetric  filling","s"]]}}
